  "content": {
    "id": "p24-q27-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "27",
    "prompt": "The lines $y=ax+11$ and $y=\\frac{1}{2}x-3$ are perpendicular.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p24-q27-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find the value of $a$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$a=-2$",
          "worked": "Perpendicular gradients have product $-1$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}a\\times\\frac{1}{2}&=-1\\\\a&=-2\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      },
      {
        "id": "p24-q27-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Find the coordinates of the point of intersection of the lines.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\left(\\frac{28}{5},-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)$\n\nSubstitute between the two line equations to find their intersection.",
          "worked": "Using $a=-2$, substitute $y=-2x+11$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\\begin{align*}-2x+11&=\\frac{1}{2}x-3\\\\-4x+22&=x-6\\\\28&=5x\\\\x&=\\frac{28}{5}\\end{align*}$$\n\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=\\frac{1}{2}\\times\\frac{28}{5}-3\\\\y&=\\frac{14}{5}-\\frac{15}{5}\\\\y&=-\\frac{1}{5}\\end{align*}$$\n\nCheck in the first equation: $-2\\times\\frac{28}{5}+11=-\\frac{1}{5}$.\n\nThe point of intersection is $\\left(\\frac{28}{5},-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 64
      }
    ]
  },
